# .gitignore
# ──────────
# ── DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED: the generated artifacts ──
# `data/raw/*.csv`, `data/processed/*.csv` and `models/saved_models/*.xgb` are
# committed on purpose. They were excluded once, for a reason that turned out to
# be wrong: "exactly reproducible from SEED=42, so the recipe is committed
# instead of the output."
#
# The seed is fixed, but reproducibility from a seed only holds while the
# generator is byte-identical. Changing how many random draws it consumes
# reshuffles the entire downstream stream, so a later generator at the same seed
# produces DIFFERENT data — same distributions, different accounts and different
# counts. The recipe is therefore not a substitute for the output, and treating
# it as one is how prose ends up quoting measurements of data nobody can
# regenerate.
#
# The deciding reason is a reader's first ten minutes. Without these files a
# fresh clone has no data and no booster, so `uvicorn api.main:app` cannot load a
# model and the dashboard cannot score until three pipeline commands have run and
# every dependency is installed. ~23 MB buys a clone that works on arrival.
#
# models/saved_models/metrics.json is committed for the same reason and one more:
# it is the published evidence for every number in the README, and both the
# dashboard and the API read it, so a clone without it cannot show a single
# metric. Committing generated evidence is only safe because staleness is
# *guarded* — v2 shipped a metrics.json advertising ROC-AUC 0.9999 for a model
# that had already been retired, and nothing caught it. Now
# tests/test_baselines.py::TestMetricsAreNotStale::
# test_metrics_describe_the_current_model_version fails the build the moment the
# file's model_version drifts from features.MODEL_VERSION, and a second test
# recomputes the leakage claim against the shipped CSVs. Keep both tests if you
# keep these files committed.

# pyvis writes its bundled vis-network/tom-select JS beside any saved HTML.
# Vendored third-party assets, regenerated on every dashboard run.
lib/

# Local working notes and run logs for the retrain-and-publish sequence. Useful
# while shipping, but scaffolding rather than part of the project, so they stay out
# of the repo.
PUBLISH-RUNBOOK.md
ANTIGRAVITY-PROMPT.md
retrain-log.txt
SUBMISSION-FORM-ANSWERS.md
SUBMISSION-CHECKLIST.md
VIDEO-SCRIPT.md

# Personal documents that live in this folder only because this folder is also
# the working directory. A resume is not part of the project and must never be
# committed — it carries a phone number and an email address into a public repo.
*.docx

